0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","3.7",{"topic":{"id":"3.7","title":"Introduction to Digital Forensics","minutes":12,"summary":"Digital forensics applies systematic, reproducible examination to digital material while preserving integrity and explaining uncertainty.","sections":[{"title":"Forensic science, not tool operation","body":["Digital forensics concerns the identification, preservation, examination, analysis, and presentation of digital information for a legal or investigative purpose. Running a recovery program is only one possible operation. The forensic quality comes from authority, method, documentation, validation, and an explanation that can be tested by another examiner.","Locard’s exchange principle is often used as an analogy for digital traces, but not every action leaves a recoverable artifact and not every artifact has the meaning it first appears to have. Logging may be disabled, clocks may differ, and data may be synchronized. Use scientific reasoning rather than treating a tool’s output as unquestionable truth."]},{"title":"Principles and repeatability","body":["Preserve the original where practicable, examine verified working copies, and document necessary changes. Use methods appropriate to the question and explain their limitations. Maintain continuity from source to result. A second examiner should be able to understand the important steps and reach a comparable observation, even if proprietary software prevents exact interface replication.","Validation asks whether a tool or method performs as claimed for the relevant task. Verification asks whether a particular acquisition or copy matches the reference. These concepts are complementary. A validated tool can be misconfigured, and a matching hash does not validate every analytical interpretation produced by the tool."]},{"title":"Form and test hypotheses","body":["Translate the investigative question into expected artifacts. If an attachment was opened, consider application records, recent-file references, browser downloads, and temporary files. If a device was connected, consider system logs and device identifiers. Seek both supporting and contrary evidence and record why artifacts may be absent.","Distinguish event time, recorded time, and acquisition time. State confidence and alternatives. A forensic conclusion can be useful without being absolute: “These artifacts are consistent with the file being opened under this account” may be the strongest supportable statement. Overstatement can damage both the case and the examiner’s credibility."]}],"practice":{"title":"From question to artifact","scenario":"You must assess whether a spreadsheet was opened before a disputed email was sent.","steps":["Write the event and time window precisely.","List expected artifacts and their limitations.","Define a contrary observation that would weaken the hypothesis.","Plan analysis on a verified copy and preserve source references."],"debrief":"A forensic method is driven by a question and testable expectations, not by opening every artifact a tool happens to display."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","bsa"],"labId":"hash","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain forensic science, not tool operation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain principles and repeatability and apply it to an investigation.","Explain form and test hypotheses and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.7","prompt":"What makes an examination forensic?","options":["A commercial software logo.","A lawful, documented, reliable, and reviewable process.","The examiner’s confidence alone.","The number of recovered files."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forensic quality comes from method, integrity, documentation, and accountable interpretation—not a product label.","unit":3}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
